WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.100
- My question to you is
have you ever said something

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.930
to your kid that you
later wish that you hadn't

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:07.670
because they repeated it?

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:10.050
And then how do you get
your kids to understand

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.110
that sometimes, things that
are said in the household

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:14.653
need to stay in the household?

00:00:14.653 --> 00:00:15.560
(laughter)
- Oh God.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.010
When you know that answer,

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.710
let me know.
- Yeah, let us know.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:20.453
- I'm going to be honest.

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:22.150
I told my, I just thought of this.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.570
I thought I don't really
have anything for that.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:26.840
And then I, my daughter became obsessed

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.200
when she was like four years
old about where she came from.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.980
And I was like, "Well,"

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:33.900
I was like, "You were."

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.480
And then she started
being nervous about death.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.210
And I was like, what is happening?

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.720
She was at four.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.320
I feel like that didn't happen
like 10 for me, but whatever.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.880
But anyway, she's advanced

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.140
and she was talking about
death and other things

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.820
and I want her to feel,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.750
good about not dying
or anything like that.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.050
And then also, "Well,
you were part of Mommy."

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.917
And then, you know, "So
you'll always have me.

00:00:54.917 --> 00:00:56.127
"So even if Mommy dies, I'm,

00:00:56.127 --> 00:00:58.747
"you were a part of me, you
were in Mommy's tummy actually.

00:00:58.747 --> 00:01:00.227
"So like, you are always a part of me.

00:01:00.227 --> 00:01:01.717
"And like, so that'll never go away.

00:01:01.717 --> 00:01:02.727
"You're from my body.

00:01:02.727 --> 00:01:04.127
"So like you carry Mommy on.

00:01:04.127 --> 00:01:06.857
"So, you know, in a sense,
Mommy will never die with you

00:01:06.857 --> 00:01:08.220
"'cause I'm a part of you."

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.190
And then she took that as in,

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.680
any woman in the world

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.330
regardless of you have a stomach or not,

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.810
walks up to and goes, "So
you have a baby in there?"

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:18.643
- Oh yeah.

00:01:18.643 --> 00:01:21.750
- [Kelly] And just
doesn't matter who it is

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.867
and just is very excited that,

00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:24.937
"Don't worry.

00:01:24.937 --> 00:01:28.250
"If you die, she's still
gonna carry you on."

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:31.140
Like, it's still, and
it's like, you missed the,

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.460
we shouldn't tell people
that they're pregnant.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:34.720
That's another one you should.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.290
Yeah, that happened. A lot.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:37.123
- You know what?

00:01:37.123 --> 00:01:38.700
My four year old was obsessed with death.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.060
Both of our kids were
obsessed with death and they,

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.188
when you're trying to explain
it, you've got to simplify it

00:01:43.188 --> 00:01:45.330
because it's too much for them to handle.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:48.760
So we had to explain that
Grandpa Greg had died

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:49.930
and was no longer with us.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.570
And then they were like, "Why?"

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.759
And we're like, "'Cause he
ate too many cheeseburgers."

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:54.592
(laughing)

00:01:54.592 --> 00:01:56.240
Because you can't,

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.470
if you go down the list
of ailments he had,

00:01:58.470 --> 00:01:59.303
he'd be be there.

00:01:59.303 --> 00:02:00.410
They'd be lose focus.

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.600
So then like every time we go somewhere,

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.110
they call them Dead Grandpa Greg.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:05.593
And we feel like that's--

00:02:05.593 --> 00:02:08.740
(laughing)
- Oh my God!

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.760
- And you know, we're too--

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.289
- Dead Grandpa Greg.
- It's accurate.

00:02:12.289 --> 00:02:14.580
- It's accurate.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:15.550
They're not wrong.
- Cheeseburgers.

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:17.660
- He had an appetite for a lot of things.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.300
- Oh my gosh!
- My kids know that

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.810
there's no baby in here

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.900
but they'll come when I'm
wearing my relaxing pants,

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.610
my loose evening pants and
go, "There's a baby in there."

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:29.443
- Oh my God.
- Gosh.

00:02:29.443 --> 00:02:30.276
- It's a food baby.

00:02:30.276 --> 00:02:31.109
It's not--
- First of all,

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:32.130
can we talk that you just said

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.840
loose, relaxing, what did you call them?

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.559
Your loose, evening comfy pants?

00:02:35.559 --> 00:02:37.280
(all laughing)

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.270
I love that!

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:39.830
My loose evening pants.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:40.663
So it's like, I love you.

00:02:40.663 --> 00:02:41.872
Your house is fancy.

00:02:41.872 --> 00:02:44.450
- She has a very fancy name for 'em.

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:45.395
- I know!

00:02:45.395 --> 00:02:46.228
(overlapping chatter)

00:02:46.228 --> 00:02:48.403
- My Lululemon's that I don't do yoga in.

